  "description": "Compute exact Jaccard similarity and estimate overlap using MinHash permutation signatures.\n\nMinHash maps high-dimensional set shingles into compact signature vectors, enabling rapid O(K) pairwise similarity queries in web-scale pre-training datasets.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nJ(A, B) = \\frac{|A \\cap B|}{|A \\cup B|}, \\quad P(h_{\\min}(A) = h_{\\min}(B)) = J(A, B)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Calculate true set intersection over union (Jaccard similarity)\n2. Evaluate minimum hash values across universal hash functions h(x) = (a x + b) mod p for sets A and B\n3. Estimate Jaccard overlap as proportion of matching MinHash signatures\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'true_jaccard', 'estimated_jaccard', 'signatures_a', and 'signatures_b'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Text Deduplication (MinHash)\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Text Deduplication (MinHash)** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nMinHash maps high-dimensional set shingles into compact signature vectors, enabling rapid O(K) pairwise similarity queries in web-scale pre-training datasets. In this problem, the goal is to: compute exact jaccard similarity and estimate overlap using minhash permutation signatures.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nJ(A, B) = \\frac{|A \\cap B|}{|A \\cup B|}, \\quad P(h_{\\min}(A) = h_{\\min}(B)) = J(A, B)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Calculate true set intersection over union (Jaccard similarity)\n2. **Phase 2**: Evaluate minimum hash values across universal hash functions h(x) = (a x + b) mod p for sets A and B\n3. **Phase 3**: Estimate Jaccard overlap as proportion of matching MinHash signatures\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: set_a = [1, 2, 3, 4], set_b = [2, 3, 4, 5], hash_coeffs = [[3, 5, 11], [7, 1, 13]]\n\nOutput: {\"true_jaccard\": 0.6, \"estimated_jaccard\": 1.0, \"signatures_a\": [0, 2], \"signatures_b\": [0, 2]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated nlp_text_deduplication_minhash under standard test conditions."
